{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.b732d8de4b","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-list-14","passage":"<p>The exchange program partners with schools in Kyoto, Japan; Seville, ______ Accra, Ghana.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Spain and","Spain: and","Spain, and","Spain; and"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Each item in this list already contains a comma, so <b>semicolons</b> must separate the items to keep them distinct. The pattern set by the first semicolon must continue before “and.”"},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.b99060b2a6","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-iq-4","passage":"<p>The committee is investigating why the project went over ______ report is expected next month.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["budget? its","budget. Its","budget? Its","budget, its"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The first sentence reports a question indirectly (for example, “wondered why” or “asked whether”) instead of asking it directly, so it ends with a <b>period</b>, not a question mark. A comma would create a comma splice."}
